4:title: "Cofinal subset of an ordinal"
33:## Definition
34:
35:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). A subset $C \subseteq \alpha$ is
36:**cofinal in $\alpha$**, equivalently **unbounded in $\alpha$**, when
37:
38:$$\text{for every } \xi \in \alpha \text{ there is } \eta \in C \text{ with } \xi \le \eta.$$
39:
40:A subset that is not cofinal is **bounded below $\alpha$**: there is
41:$\xi \in \alpha$ such that $\eta < \xi$ for every $\eta \in C$.
42:
===== def-cofinality =====
4:title: "Cofinality $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha)$, and regular and singular cardinals"
39:## Definition
40:
41:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\alpha$ is
42:
43:$$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \;:=\; \text{the least ordinal } \beta \text{ for which some } f : \beta \to \alpha \text{ has cofinal range},$$
44:
45:cofinal range meaning that $f[\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\alpha$
46:([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\zeta \in \alpha$ satisfies
47:$\zeta \le f(\xi)$ for some $\xi \in \beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and
48:that a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is
49:[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So
50:$\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.
51:
52:**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\kappa$
53:([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is
54:
55:- **regular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa$;
56:- **singular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) \ne \kappa$.
57:
58:The two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by
59:[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly
60:$\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) < \kappa$, since $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$
61:always holds.
62:
===== def-complete-ordered-field =====
4:title: "Complete ordered field (least-upper-bound property)"
29:## Definition
30:
31:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq F$.
32:
33:- $u \in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$; $S$ is
34:  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.
35:- $u \in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$
36:  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.
37:
38:$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the
39:**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty
40:$S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.
41:
===== def-countable =====
4:title: "Finite, countably infinite, countable, uncountable"
43:## Definition
44:
45:Recall that a natural number is a von Neumann natural
46:([[def-natural-numbers]]): $0 = \varnothing$ and $\sigma(n) = n \cup \{n\}$, so
47:that
48:
49:$$n = \{\, m \in \mathbb{N} : m < n \,\} = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$$
50:
51:is itself the set of its predecessors. Here $<$ is the order of
52:[[def-nat-order]], which is defined additively, so the displayed identity is a
53:theorem and not a convention: it is [[lem-nat-order-is-membership]], proved
54:immediately above. Let $A$ be a set, and let $\approx$ be equinumerosity
55:([[def-equinumerous]]).
56:
57:- $A$ is **finite** if $A \approx n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
58:- $A$ is **countably infinite** if $A \approx \mathbb{N}$.
59:- $A$ is **at most countable** if it is finite or countably infinite.
60:- $A$ is **uncountable** if it is not at most countable.
61:
===== def-equinumerous =====
4:title: "Equinumerous sets, $A \\approx B$ and $A \\preceq B$"
41:## Definition
42:
43:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] for the
44:terminology).
45:
46:- $A$ and $B$ are **equinumerous**, written $A \approx B$, if there exists a
47:  bijection $f : A \to B$.
48:- $A$ is **dominated by** $B$, written $A \preceq B$, if there exists an
49:  injection $f : A \to B$.
50:- $A \prec B$ abbreviates: $A \preceq B$ and not $A \approx B$.
51:
===== def-first-uncountable-ordinal =====
4:title: "The first uncountable ordinal $\\omega_1 := \\aleph(\\omega)$"
34:## Definition
35:
36:The **first uncountable ordinal** is
37:
38:$$\omega_1 \;:=\; \aleph(\omega),$$
39:
40:the Hartogs number of $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ ([[thm-hartogs]],
41:[[def-natural-numbers]]): the least ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]) that admits no
42:injective function into $\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently, by that theorem,
43:$\omega_1$ is the set of order types of the well-ordered subsets of
44:$\mathbb{N}$.
45:
46:**Existence is a theorem of ZF.** [[thm-hartogs]] is choice free, so $\omega_1$
47:is available without any choice principle, and its defining property needs
48:none either.
49:
50:**"Uncountable" is [[def-countable]]'s word**, meaning "not at most countable",
51:and it is not redefined here. That $\omega_1$ deserves the name — that it is
52:uncountable, that every ordinal below it is at most countable, that it is a
53:cardinal and a limit ordinal — is proved in
54:[[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]], which is what discharges the
55:naming obligation of this definition.
56:
===== def-injection-surjection-bijection =====
4:title: "Injection, surjection, bijection"
37:## Definition
38:
39:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets and let $f : A \to B$ be a function.
40:
41:- $f$ is **injective** (one-to-one) if $f(x) = f(y)$ implies $x = y$, for all
42:  $x, y \in A$.
43:- $f$ is **surjective** (onto) if for every $b \in B$ there is some $x \in A$
44:  with $f(x) = b$; equivalently, the image $f[A] := \{ f(x) : x \in A \}$ equals
45:  $B$.
46:- $f$ is **bijective** if it is both injective and surjective.
47:
48:For $S \subseteq A$ we write $f[S] = \{ f(x) : x \in S \}$ for the image of $S$,
49:and for $T \subseteq B$ we write $f^{-1}[T] = \{ x \in A : f(x) \in T \}$ for the
50:preimage of $T$.
51:
===== def-limit-ordinal =====
4:title: "Successor and limit ordinals"
33:## Definition
34:
35:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]).
36:
37:- $\alpha$ is a **successor ordinal** when $\alpha = \beta^{+} = \beta \cup \{\beta\}$
38:  for some ordinal $\beta$, which is then an ordinal by [[lem-ordinal-basics]];
39:- $\alpha$ is a **limit ordinal** when $\alpha \ne 0$ and $\alpha$ is not a
40:  successor ordinal.
41:
42:Every ordinal is therefore exactly one of: $0$, a successor ordinal, or a limit
43:ordinal. The three cases are the three clauses of a definition or a proof by
44:transfinite recursion or induction over the ordinals.
45:
===== def-natural-numbers =====
4:title: "The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann)"
31:## Definition
32:
33:The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**
34:([[def-inductive-set]]),
35:
36:$$\mathbb{N} = \omega := \bigcap\,\{\, I : I \text{ is inductive} \,\},$$
37:
38:which exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the
39:Axiom of Infinity supplies one inductive set to intersect within, and Separation
40:makes the intersection a set). On $\mathbb{N}$ we take
41:
42:$$0 := \varnothing, \qquad \sigma(n) := n \cup \{n\},$$
43:
44:the distinguished element and the successor function. Thus
45:$0 = \varnothing$, $1 = \{0\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, $3 = \{0, 1, 2\}$, and in general
46:$n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is the set of its predecessors.
47:

