===== lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws =====
4:title: "Commutativity, associativity, distributivity and monotonicity of $\\oplus$ and $\\otimes$, the unit laws, the two exponent laws, and $\\kappa \\le \\lambda$ if and only if $\\kappa$ injects into $\\lambda$"
40:## Statement
41:
42:Let $\kappa$, $\lambda$, $\mu$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]) and let
43:$\oplus$, $\otimes$, $\kappa^{\lambda}$ be as in [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]].
44:Clauses (a) to (e) are theorems of ZF; the clauses naming an exponential hold
45:whenever those exponentials are defined, in particular under the Axiom of Choice
46:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
47:
48:**(a) Comparison.** $\kappa \le \lambda$ if and only if $\kappa \preceq \lambda$,
49:that is, if and only if there is an injection $\kappa \to \lambda$
50:([[def-equinumerous]]). More generally, if $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable and
51:$A \preceq B$ then $\lvert A \rvert \le \lvert B \rvert$.
52:
53:**(b) Commutativity and associativity.**
54:$\kappa \oplus \lambda = \lambda \oplus \kappa$,
55:$\kappa \otimes \lambda = \lambda \otimes \kappa$,
56:$(\kappa \oplus \lambda) \oplus \mu = \kappa \oplus (\lambda \oplus \mu)$ and
57:$(\kappa \otimes \lambda) \otimes \mu = \kappa \otimes (\lambda \otimes \mu)$.
58:
59:**(c) Distributivity.**
60:$\kappa \otimes (\lambda \oplus \mu) = (\kappa \otimes \lambda) \oplus (\kappa \otimes \mu)$.
61:
62:**(d) Units.** $\kappa \oplus 0 = \kappa$, $\kappa \otimes 0 = 0$,
63:$\kappa \otimes 1 = \kappa$, and $\kappa^{0} = 1$, $\kappa^{1} = \kappa$,
64:$1^{\kappa} = 1$, together with $0^{\kappa} = 0$ for $\kappa \ne 0$. The four
65:exponential unit laws need no choice principle, because the function sets they
66:count are empty, a singleton, or a copy of $\kappa$.
67:
68:**(e) Monotonicity.** If $\kappa \le \lambda$ then
69:$\kappa \oplus \mu \le \lambda \oplus \mu$ and
70:$\kappa \otimes \mu \le \lambda \otimes \mu$; and
71:$\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$, and $\mu^{\kappa} \le \mu^{\lambda}$ provided
72:$\mu \ne 0$.
73:
74:**(f) The two exponent laws.**
75:$\kappa^{\lambda \oplus \mu} = \kappa^{\lambda} \otimes \kappa^{\mu}$ and
76:$(\kappa^{\lambda})^{\mu} = \kappa^{\lambda \otimes \mu}$.
77:
78:**Each clause is an equality or an inequality of cardinals**, not merely of sizes: each side is
79:an ordinal, and the claim is that the two ordinals are the same.
80:
===== lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined =====
4:title: "Disjoint union, cartesian product, function space and power set respect equinumerosity, and for ordinals $\\alpha, \\beta$ the sets $\\alpha \\sqcup \\beta$ and $\\alpha \\times \\beta$ carry explicit well-orders, so their cardinalities exist in ZF"
40:## Statement
41:
42:For sets $A$ and $B$ write
43:
44:$$A \sqcup B := (\{0\} \times A) \cup (\{1\} \times B), \qquad {}^{B}A := \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } B \to A \,\},$$
45:
46:so $A \sqcup B$ is the **disjoint union**, made disjoint by tagging, and
47:${}^{B}A$ is the set of all functions from $B$ to $A$. Work in ZF. Then:
48:
49:**(a) Representative independence.** If $A \approx A'$ and $B \approx B'$
50:([[def-equinumerous]]) then
51:
52:$$A \sqcup B \approx A' \sqcup B', \qquad A \times B \approx A' \times B', \qquad {}^{B}A \approx {}^{B'}A'.$$
53:
54:**(b) Power sets.** If $A \approx B$ then $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(B)$.
55:
56:**(c) Two operations are choice-free.** For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$
57:([[def-ordinal]]) the sets $\alpha \sqcup \beta$ and $\alpha \times \beta$ carry
58:**explicitly defined** well-orders ([[def-well-order]]), so each is equinumerous
59:with an ordinal and each has a cardinality
60:$\lvert \alpha \sqcup \beta \rvert$, $\lvert \alpha \times \beta \rvert$
61:in ZF ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
62:
63:**(d) The third is not.** Nothing here well-orders ${}^{\beta}\alpha$, and no
64:argument on this page does. Assuming the Axiom of Choice
65:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is well-orderable
66:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and ${}^{\beta}\alpha$ has a cardinality like any
67:other set; that is where cardinal exponentiation gets its hypothesis.
68:
===== lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set =====
4:title: "A set equinumerous with some ordinal has a least such ordinal, that ordinal is a cardinal, and equinumerous sets get the same one; no choice principle is used"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Call a set $X$ **well-orderable** when some relation well-orders it
45:([[def-well-order]]). Work in ZF, with no choice principle. Then:
46:
47:**(a)** $X$ is well-orderable if and only if $X \approx \alpha$
48:([[def-equinumerous]]) for some ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]).
49:
50:**(b)** If $X$ is well-orderable there is a **least** ordinal equinumerous with
51:$X$. It is written $\lvert X \rvert$ and called the **cardinality** of $X$.
52:
53:**(c)** $\lvert X \rvert$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]).
54:
55:**(d)** If $X \approx Y$ and $X$ is well-orderable, then $Y$ is well-orderable
56:and $\lvert Y \rvert = \lvert X \rvert$.
57:
58:**(e)** $\lvert \alpha \rvert \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$, and
59:$\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal.
60:
61:Assuming the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is
62:well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), so $\lvert X \rvert$ is then
63:defined for every set and is exactly the cardinality of [[def-cardinal]].
64:
65:**Why this item exists.** [[def-cardinal]] introduces $\lvert X \rvert$ under the
66:hypothesis "Assume the Axiom of Choice", and it needs that hypothesis only to
67:know that $X$ carries a well-order at all. Everything below is about
68:well-orderable sets and is a theorem of ZF, which is what makes it possible to
69:state Hessenberg's theorem and Tarski's theorem, one of which is choice-free and
70:the other of which is precisely about the gap between ZF and ZFC.
71:
===== lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n =====
4:title: "A nonempty set is at most countable iff it is a surjective image of $\\mathbb{N}$"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $A$ be a nonempty set. Then $A$ is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) if
45:and only if there is a surjection $s : \mathbb{N} \to A$
46:([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
47:
48:Moreover, from any such surjection an injection $A \to \mathbb{N}$ is obtained
49:explicitly, without any choice, by
50:
51:$$j(a) := \min\{\, k \in \mathbb{N} : s(k) = a \,\}.$$
52:
53:This is the working form of countability used everywhere below: to prove a
54:nonempty set countable it suffices to list its elements, repetitions and all.
55:
56:**No choice principle is used.** The backward direction is where an appeal to
57:choice would be natural ("for each $a$ pick some $k$ with $s(k) = a$") and it is
58:avoided outright, because $\min$ is canonical: every nonempty set of naturals has
59:a *least* element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]), so $j(a)$ is determined by
60:$a$ and $s$ alone.
61:
===== lem-of-q-dense =====
4:title: "ℚ is dense in every Archimedean ordered field"
30:## Statement
31:
32:Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let
33:$\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then
34:$\iota(\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational
35:$q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$.
36:
===== lem-of-q-embeds =====
4:title: "The unique embedding of ℚ into an ordered field"
34:## Statement
35:
36:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). There is a unique field
37:homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ ([[def-field-homomorphism]]). On the
38:integers it is given by $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ (with $-n \mapsto -(n \cdot 1_F)$
39:and $0 \mapsto 0$), and on a rational written as $p/q$ with $q \ge 1$ by
40:$\iota(p/q) = \iota(p)\,(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$. Moreover $\iota$ is injective and
41:order-preserving, so it is an embedding of $\mathbb{Q}$ as an ordered subfield of
42:$F$, and it is the only field homomorphism $\mathbb{Q} \to F$.
43:
===== lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal =====
4:title: "$\\omega$ is the least limit ordinal"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ be the natural numbers ([[def-natural-numbers]]) with
45:their usual order ([[def-nat-order]]). Then:
46:
47:**(i)** for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$, $m \in n$ if and only if $m < n$;
48:
49:**(ii)** every natural number is an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]), and $\omega$ is an
50:ordinal;
51:
52:**(iii)** $\omega$ is a limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]);
53:
54:**(iv)** every ordinal $\alpha \in \omega$ is $0$ or a successor ordinal, and
55:consequently $\omega$ is the **least** limit ordinal: $\omega \subseteq \gamma$,
56:that is $\omega \le \gamma$, for every limit ordinal $\gamma$.
57:
58:So the natural numbers are exactly the ordinals below $\omega$, and $\omega$ is
59:the first ordinal at which induction acquires a limit clause.
60:
61:**Everything here is a theorem of ZF, and no choice principle is used.** The
62:only axiom beyond the basic ones that any of it needs is Infinity, which is what
63:makes $\omega$ a set at all ([[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]]).
64:
===== lem-ordinal-basics =====
4:title: "Basic closure properties of ordinals"
36:## Statement
37:
38:Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be ordinals ([[def-ordinal]]). Then:
39:
40:**(a)** every element of $\alpha$ is an ordinal;
41:
42:**(b)** $\alpha \notin \alpha$;
43:
44:**(c)** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal;
45:
46:**(d)** if $A$ is a **nonempty** set of ordinals then $\bigcap A$ is an ordinal;
47:
48:**(e)** if $A$ is any set of ordinals then $\bigcup A$ is an ordinal;
49:
50:**(f)** $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ if and only if $\alpha \in \beta$ or
51:$\alpha = \beta$;
52:
53:**(g)** any two ordinals are comparable under inclusion: $\alpha \subseteq \beta$
54:or $\beta \subseteq \alpha$.
55:
56:Everything here is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.
57:
===== lem-ordinal-trichotomy =====
4:title: "Trichotomy and well-ordering of the ordinals"
40:## Statement
41:
42:For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$ ([[def-ordinal]]), **exactly one** of
43:
44:$$\alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha = \beta, \qquad \beta \in \alpha$$
45:
46:holds. Moreover every nonempty **set** $A$ of ordinals has an $\in$-least
47:element, and consequently $A$ ordered by
48:$\alpha \le \beta :\iff \alpha \subseteq \beta$ is a well-order
49:([[def-well-order]]) whose strict part is membership.
50:
51:So the ordinals are linearly ordered by $\in$, every set of them is well
52:ordered, and [[thm-transfinite-induction]] is available on any such set. No
53:choice principle is used.
54:
===== lem-recursion-on-the-ordinals =====
4:title: "Transfinite recursion along the ordinals: a class rule determines exactly one operation defined at every ordinal"
39:## Statement
40:
41:Let $G$ be a **class function**: a rule, given by a formula in the language of
42:set theory, that assigns a set $G(h)$ to every function $h$ whose domain is an
43:ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). Then there is a class function $F$, given by a
44:formula and defined at **every** ordinal, such that
45:
46:$$F(\beta) = G(F \restriction \beta) \qquad \text{for every ordinal } \beta,$$
47:
48:and $F$ is the only one: any class function $F'$ defined at every ordinal and
49:satisfying $F'(\beta) = G(F' \restriction \beta)$ for every ordinal $\beta$
50:agrees with $F$ at every ordinal. Here $F \restriction \beta$ is the restriction
51:of $F$ to the set $\beta$ of ordinals below $\beta$, which is a set even though
52:$F$ is not.
53:
54:Like [[thm-transfinite-recursion]], this is a **theorem schema** of ZF: one
55:theorem for each formula defining $G$. **It uses Replacement, inherited from
56:that theorem, and it uses no form of the Axiom of Choice.**
57:
58:**Why the published theorem does not already say this.**
59:[[thm-transfinite-recursion]] is stated for a well-order $(W, <)$, that is for a
60:**set**, and it delivers one function whose domain is that set. An operation
61:such as $\alpha + \beta$ has to be defined at every ordinal $\beta$, and the
62:ordinals are not a set ([[thm-burali-forti]]), so no single instance of the
63:published theorem defines it. What is proved below is exactly the bridge: the
64:instances at the individual ordinals cohere, and the coherence is supplied by
65:the published theorem's own **uniqueness** clause. No new recursion principle is
66:introduced.
67:

