40:## Statement
41:
42:Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. Let $\operatorname{cf}$ be the
43:cofinality of [[def-cofinality]]. Then:
44:
45:**(a)** $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$
46:([[def-ordinal]]);
47:
48:**(b)** $\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$, and $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha + 1) = 1$ for
49:every ordinal $\alpha$, where $\alpha + 1 = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$
50:([[def-ordinal-addition]]);
51:
52:**(c)** for a limit ordinal $\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]),
53:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is an **infinite cardinal** ([[def-cardinal]]) and
54:$\operatorname{cf}(\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)) = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$, so
55:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is a **regular** cardinal;
56:
57:**(d)** for a limit ordinal $\lambda$, every cofinal $C \subseteq \lambda$
58:([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]) satisfies
59:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \lvert C \rvert$, and some cofinal subset of
60:$\lambda$ has cardinality exactly $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$.
61:
62:Clause (c) is what discharges the naming obligation of [[def-cofinality]]:
63:"regular" is defined through $\operatorname{cf}$, and it is a theorem, not a
64:convention, that $\operatorname{cf}$ of a limit ordinal is a cardinal at which
65:the definition can be tested.
66:
===== thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded =====
4:title: "Assuming countable choice: every at most countable subset of $\\omega_1$ is bounded below $\\omega_1$, so no at most countable subset of $\\omega_1$ is cofinal in it, and a supremum of at most countably many at most countable ordinals is at most countable"
37:## Statement
38:
39:**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$**
40:([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\omega_1$ be the first uncountable ordinal
41:([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]). Then:
42:
43:**(a) Boundedness.** Every at most countable ([[def-countable]]) subset
44:$A \subseteq \omega_1$ is bounded below $\omega_1$: the ordinal
45:$\sup A = \bigcup A$ lies in $\omega_1$ and satisfies $\alpha \le \sup A$ for
46:every $\alpha \in A$.
47:
48:**(b) No small cofinal set.** No at most countable subset of $\omega_1$ is
49:cofinal in $\omega_1$ ([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]).
50:
51:**(c) Suprema stay countable.** If $A$ is an at most countable set of at most
52:countable ordinals, then $\sup A = \bigcup A$ is an at most countable ordinal.
53:
54:**The hypothesis is not decoration.** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is spent at exactly
55:one step, step 1.2 below, and it is spent there only through
56:[[thm-countable-union-of-countable]], whose own statement carries the same
57:hypothesis. Everything else on this page, including the existence of $\omega_1$
58:and all of [[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]], is a theorem of
59:ZF. The ledger is the choice-ledger remark at the end of this page.
60:
===== thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph =====
4:title: "Every infinite cardinal is $\\aleph_\\alpha$ for exactly one ordinal $\\alpha$, in ZF; and, assuming the Axiom of Choice, every infinite set is equinumerous with exactly one aleph"
40:## Statement
41:
42:**(a) In ZF.** Every infinite cardinal $\kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]]) equals
43:$\aleph_\alpha$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) for **exactly one** ordinal
44:$\alpha$. So the alephs are not merely a supply of infinite cardinals: they are
45:all of them, and the operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ is a bijective
46:enumeration of the infinite cardinals by the ordinals.
47:
48:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Every infinite
49:set is equinumerous ([[def-equinumerous]]) with exactly one aleph.
50:
51:The two clauses say different things, and the difference is the whole content of
52:the choice hypothesis. Clause (a) classifies **cardinals**, which are ordinals;
53:clause (b) classifies **sets**, and needs to know first that an arbitrary set has
54:a cardinality at all.
55:
===== thm-hessenberg =====
4:title: "Hessenberg: $\\kappa \\otimes \\kappa = \\kappa$ for every infinite cardinal $\\kappa$, proved in ZF from the canonical well-order of $\\kappa \\times \\kappa$"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $\kappa$ be an **infinite cardinal**, that is a cardinal
45:([[def-cardinal]]) with $\omega \le \kappa$. Then
46:
47:$$\kappa \otimes \kappa = \kappa, \qquad \text{equivalently} \qquad \lvert \kappa \times \kappa \rvert = \kappa$$
48:
49:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
50:
51:**This is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.** The well-order that
52:carries the proof is written down from the ordinal order on $\kappa$; nothing is
53:selected anywhere. That matters for this page: Hessenberg's theorem is exactly
54:the part of "an infinite set is the same size as its square" that survives
55:without choice.
56:
===== thm-n-cross-n-countable =====
4:title: "$\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N} \\approx \\mathbb{N}$"
42:## Statement
43:
44:$\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the
45:plane of pairs of naturals is countably infinite ([[def-countable]]).
46:
47:The bijection is exhibited, not merely asserted to exist. Define $2^m$ by
48:recursion on $m$ ([[thm-recursion]]) by $2^0 = 1$ and $2^{\sigma(m)} = 2^m + 2^m$,
49:and set
50:
51:$$J(m,n) = 2^m \cdot \sigma(n + n), \qquad \text{that is} \qquad J(m,n) = 2^m(2n+1).$$
52:
53:Then $J$ is a bijection from $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ onto
54:$\mathbb{N} \setminus \{0\}$, and $\sigma$ is a bijection from $\mathbb{N}$ onto
55:$\mathbb{N} \setminus \{0\}$, so $\sigma^{-1} \circ J$ is a bijection
56:$\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$. What makes $J$ bijective is the
57:decomposition of a nonzero natural into a power of two times an odd number,
58:existence and uniqueness both.
59:
===== thm-of-archimedean =====
4:title: "Every complete ordered field is Archimedean"
34:## Statement
35:
36:Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:
37:for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$,
38:where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$
39:([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.
40:
===== thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal =====
4:title: "$\\omega_1$ is uncountable, every ordinal below it is at most countable, it is a cardinal and a limit ordinal, and its existence is a theorem of ZF"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $\omega_1 = \aleph(\omega)$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]). Then:
38:
39:**(a) The bridge.** An ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]) injects into
40:$\mathbb{N}$ if and only if $\alpha$ is at most countable
41:([[def-countable]]).
42:
43:**(b)** $\omega_1$ is **uncountable**.
44:
45:**(c)** Every ordinal $\alpha \in \omega_1$ is at most countable; so $\omega_1$
46:is the least uncountable ordinal.
47:
48:**(d)** $\omega_1$ is a **cardinal**, that is an initial ordinal
49:([[def-cardinal]]): no $\alpha \in \omega_1$ is equinumerous with $\omega_1$.
50:
51:**(e)** $\omega_1$ is a **limit ordinal** ([[def-limit-ordinal]]).
52:
53:**All of this is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.** That matters
54:here and is stated deliberately: [[thm-hartogs]] is choice free,
55:[[lem-subset-of-countable]] and [[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]] are
56:choice free, so $\omega_1$ and every property listed above exist in ZF alone.
57:The cost begins two items later on this page, at the boundedness theorem for at
58:most countable subsets of $\omega_1$, which genuinely needs countable choice.
59:
===== thm-rationals-countable =====
4:title: "$\\mathbb{Q}$ is countably infinite"
44:## Statement
45:
46:$\mathbb{Q} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the rationals are
47:countably infinite ([[def-countable]]).
48:
49:**No choice principle is used.** The one place where a reader expects a choice,
50:"pick a representative $a/b$ of each rational", is exactly where
51:[[lem-rat-positive-denominator]] applies: every rational *has* a representative
52:with positive denominator, so the map $(a,b) \mapsto [(a,b)]$ defined on
53:$\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ is already **surjective** onto $\mathbb{Q}$,
54:and countability follows from a surjection without ever selecting a
55:representative. The same device handles $\mathbb{Z}$, which is a surjective image
56:of $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ by construction ([[def-integers]]).
57:
===== thm-schroder-bernstein =====
4:title: "The Schröder-Bernstein theorem"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets with $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$
45:([[def-equinumerous]]). Then $A \approx B$.
46:
47:Equivalently: if there is an injection $A \to B$ and an injection $B \to A$, then
48:there is a bijection $A \to B$ ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
49:
50:**The proof uses no choice principle.** The bijection is written down explicitly
51:from the two given injections, and the only "selections" it makes are of the
52:unique preimage of a point under an injection, which is determined, not chosen.
53:The single infinite construction is an application of the recursion theorem
54:([[thm-recursion]]), whose data are a set, a starting point and one function.
55:
===== thm-transfinite-induction =====
4:title: "Transfinite induction"
40:## Statement
41:
42:Let $(W, <)$ be a well-order ([[def-well-order]]) and let $S \subseteq W$
43:satisfy the following: for every $a \in W$, if $W_{<a} \subseteq S$ then
44:$a \in S$ ([[def-initial-segment]]). Then $S = W$.
45:
46:In property form: if a property $P$ of elements of $W$ satisfies "whenever
47:$P(x)$ holds for every $x < a$, it holds at $a$", then $P(a)$ holds for every
48:$a \in W$.
49:
50:**This is a theorem of ZF.** No form of the Axiom of Choice is used. Choice is
51:perfectly available at this point in the library, since Zorn's lemma is proved
52:from it on the previous page; the claim made here is about this proof, which
53:never invokes it.
54:
===== thm-well-ordering-theorem =====
4:title: "The well-ordering theorem"
46:## Statement
47:
48:Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Then **every set $X$ can
49:be well ordered**: there is a relation on $X$ making it a well-ordered set
50:([[def-well-order]]).
51:
52:The Axiom of Choice is used only inside [[thm-zorn]], and nowhere else in the
53:argument below.
54:

