    42	## Remark
    43	
    44	This item is bookkeeping, in the manner of [[rem-choice-ledger]]: it records what
    45	each result stated here actually costs, so that anything quoting a result from
    46	this page knows whether it is quoting a theorem of ZF or a consequence of the
    47	Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
    48	
    49	**Theorems of ZF, using no choice principle at all.**
    50	
    51	- [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]: a well-orderable set has a least
    52	  equinumerous ordinal, that ordinal is a cardinal, and equinumerous sets receive
    53	  the same one. The choice hypothesis of [[def-cardinal]] is needed only to make
    54	  *every* set well-orderable.
    55	- [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]], and with it $\oplus$ and
    56	  $\otimes$ of [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]. The disjoint union and the product of
    57	  two ordinals are well-ordered by orders written down from the ordinal order.
    58	- [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], the dictionary with
    59	  finite counting.
    60	- [[thm-hessenberg]] and its consequence [[cor-cardinal-absorption]].
    61	- [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]], and with it the whole aleph hierarchy:
    62	  [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]] and
    63	  [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]] in their aleph clauses, and clause (a) of
    64	  [[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]].
    65	- [[def-cofinality]] and [[thm-cofinality-basics]]; and clauses (a) and (c) of
    66	  [[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]], that $\aleph_0$ is regular and
    67	  $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0$.
    68	
    69	**Costing the Axiom of Choice, and named as such in their own statements.**
    70	
    71	- Cardinal exponentiation $\kappa^{\lambda}$ itself, because ZF does not
    72	  well-order a set of functions. Everything reached through it inherits the
    73	  hypothesis: [[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]], the beth clauses of
    74	  [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product]],
    75	  [[thm-konig]] and [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]].
    76	- Clause (b) of [[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]], that an arbitrary
    77	  infinite *set* is equinumerous with an aleph.
    78	- Clauses (b) and (d) of [[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]], that successor alephs
    79	  are regular and that $\aleph_\omega$ is the least singular infinite cardinal.
    80	
    81	**Equivalent to the Axiom of Choice over ZF**, so neither weaker nor stronger:
    82	comparability of arbitrary sets ([[thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac]]) and
    83	Tarski's square law ([[thm-tarski-square]]). The first is recorded in
    84	[[rem-choice-ledger]] as Hartogs' result, quoted there and proved here.
    85	
    86	**Countable choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]) is not used anywhere on this
    87	page. Where an argument might have needed it, the ordinal structure supplied a
    88	canonical least element instead.
    89	
    90	**How far regularity can fail without choice.** That clause (b) above cannot be
    91	proved in ZF is recorded rather than proved here, and it is conditional:
    92	[[rem-gitik-all-uncountable-cardinals-singular]] states that, relative to a
    93	large-cardinal consistency hypothesis, there is a model of ZF in which **every**
    94	uncountable cardinal is singular. Granting that hypothesis, "successor cardinals
    95	are regular" is a consequence of choice and not a structural fact about
    96	cardinals, which is why clause (b) is stated with its hypothesis.
    97	
    98	**Where the continuum escapes ZFC.** The constraint proved here is
    99	$\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0$
   100	([[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]]), which excludes some candidate values
   101	for $2^{\aleph_0}$ and selects none. Whether $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1$ is the
   102	continuum hypothesis, stated in [[rem-continuum-hypothesis]]; that ZFC proves
   103	neither it nor its negation, granted the consistency of ZFC, is recorded in
   104	[[rem-independence-of-ch-and-gch]] and is proved neither on this page nor on any
   105	page this one rests on. The
   106	generalised form is stronger than it looks: over ZF it implies the Axiom of
   107	Choice, a result of Sierpiński recorded in [[rem-gch-implies-ac]], and proved
   108	neither here nor on any page this one rests on.
   109	
   110	**What this page therefore does and does not settle about $2^{\aleph_0}$.** It
   111	settles that $2^{\aleph_0}$ is an aleph, granted choice; that it is strictly
   112	above $\aleph_0$; and that its cofinality is uncountable. It settles nothing
   113	about which aleph it is, and no statement on this page or its companion asserts a
   114	value.

